    "reading": {
        "body": "Cryptography hiring in this sample is concentrated in three employers, and one of them, IonQ, is a quantum computing company hiring for post-quantum cryptography.\n\nIonQ accounts for four of the eight postings, all in Geneva and all posted on the same day. ServiceNow accounts for three, split between Santa Clara and San Diego. Cloudflare holds the remaining one. The roles are senior and above, with titles running from senior engineer to distinguished architect, and the function is identical across every listing.\n\nThe pattern is a small number of large platform and infrastructure companies building cryptographic foundations in-house rather than buying them. For AI specifically, this matters indirectly. Training and inference run on rented compute reached over networks, and the keys, certificates, and attestation chains that secure that traffic are the same systems these roles maintain. Post-quantum migration is a multi-year rekeying of that infrastructure, and IonQ's cluster suggests the work is being staffed now rather than deferred.\n\nWhat the data does not show is volume. Eight postings across three companies over roughly a week is a narrow slice, and the source list includes aggregators that may not have been queried for this function. It says nothing about compensation, team size, or whether these are new positions or backfills. It also cannot distinguish cryptography hiring driven by AI workloads from hiring driven by general security and compliance requirements, which is the question a reader would most want answered.",
